SUMMARY:Robotics Institute Seminar: Steven Waslander\, “TankerVision: Turning Firefighting Flights into a Wildfire Behaviour Dataset”
DESCRIPTION:Join in-person (BA1190) or online (Zoom) \nTankerVision: Turning Firefighting Flights into a Wildfire Behaviour Dataset\nAbstract\nModels of wildfire rate of spread\, intensity and spotting still rely mostly on small experimental burns from decades ago. Fires are hardest to observe exactly when it matters most\, during rapid\, intense spread. Satellites are too coarse in space and time\, and drones and research aircraft are kept out of fire airspace. The only platforms reliably overhead during a major run are the aircraft fighting the fire. \nTankerVision turns these operational flights into a continuous\, automated source of fire behaviour data. Working with Conair Aerial Firefighting\, the BC Wildfire Service and Alberta Wildfire\, we developed a self-contained imaging unit for the bird-dog aircraft that direct airtanker operations. It records georeferenced colour and thermal infrared imagery whenever the aircraft is near a fire\, with no action required from the crew. A prototype flew roughly 50 missions over 30 fires from Kamloops in 2025. \nWith support from DRDC and NRCan\, three upgraded aircraft flew fires across five provinces and territories in 2026. An automated pipeline segments fire and smoke\, reconstructs georeferenced 3D fire fronts and derives rate-of-spread observations from successive passes. The result is the first large multi-pass\, multi-fire dataset of active wildfire behaviour\, a foundation for data-driven fire prediction models\, with an initial public release planned for December 2026. \nAbout Steven Waslander\nProf. Steven Waslander is a leading authority on autonomous vehicles\, including multirotor drones and autonomous driving vehicles.  He received his B.Sc.E.in 1998 from Queen’s University\, his M.S. in 2002 and his Ph.D. in 2007\, both from Stanford University in Aeronautics and Astronautics\, where as a graduate student he created the Stanford Testbed of Autonomous Rotorcraft for Multi-Agent Control (STARMAC)\, the world’s most capable outdoor multi-vehicle quadrotor platform at the time. He was recruited to Waterloo from Stanford in 2008\, where he founded and directed the Waterloo Autonomous Vehicle Laboratory (WAVELab)\, extending the state of the art in autonomous drones and autonomous driving through advances in localization and mapping\, object detection and tracking\, integrated planning and control methods and multi-robot coordination. His work on autonomous vehicles has resulted in the Autonomoose\, the first autonomous vehicle created at a Canadian University to drive on public roads. In 2018\, he joined the University of Toronto Institute for Aerospace Studies (UTIAS)\, and founded the Toronto Robotics and Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (TRAILab). Starting in July of 2026\, he became the director of the University of Toronto Robotics Institute. \nJoin in-person (BA1190) or online (Zoom)
URL:https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/robotics-institute-seminar-steven-waslander-tankervision-turning-firefighting-flights-into-a-wildfire-behaviour-dataset/
LOCATION:Bahen Centre for Information Technology\, Room 1190\, or on Zoom\, 40 St George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5S 2E4\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Lectures, seminars and workshops
END:VEVENT
